![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

In a Service Worker, when using a `stale-while-revalidate` strategy, returning the cached response immediately allows the fetch event to finish. If the background network request and cache update are not tied to the event's lifecycle using `event.waitUntil()`, the browser may terminate the service worker thread before the fetch or cache write completes.\n\nTo prevent silent cache update failures, pass the `event` object to `staleWhileRevalidate` and call `event.waitUntil(networkResponse)` when serving from cache.

```javascript
async function staleWhileRevalidate(request, event) {
  const cache = await caches.open(CACHE_NAME)
  const cached = await cache.match(request)
  const networkResponse = fetch(request)
    .then(async (response) => {
      await cacheResponse(cache, request, response)
      return response
    })
    .catch((error) => {
      if (!cached) {
        throw error
      }
      console.error("Failed to revalidate cached OCR asset:", request.url, error)
      return cached
    })
  if (cached && event) {
    event.waitUntil(networkResponse)
  }
  return cached || networkResponse
}
```

![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

Every brand match seen in a frame (including single-frame OCR hallucinations or noisy detections) is added to `this.lastMatch`. However, `evictExpired` only deletes keys that exist in `this.confirmedUntil`. Unconfirmed brands are never promoted to `this.confirmedUntil`, meaning they are never evicted from `this.lastMatch`. Over time, this causes `this.lastMatch` to grow indefinitely, leading to a memory leak.\n\nTo fix this, clean up any keys in `this.lastMatch` that are not currently confirmed at the end of `addFrame`.

```typescript
    this.promoteWinners(matches, now)
    this.evictExpired(now)
    for (const brand of this.lastMatch.keys()) {
      if (!this.confirmedUntil.has(brand)) {
        this.lastMatch.delete(brand)
      }
    }
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

If the `OcrClient` has already been disposed, handling the `onerror` event is redundant and could lead to double-termination or unexpected state transitions. It is safer to guard the error handler with a check on `this.disposed`.

```suggestion
    this.worker.onerror = (event) => {
      if (this.disposed) {
        return
      }
      this.failWorker(new Error(event.message || "OCR worker failed"))
    }
```

